And what is nuclear energy\par
\par
Nuclear energy is the energy released from the nucleus (core) of an atom. It is unlocked when the bonds that hold the subatomic particles (protons and neutrons) together are broken or rearranged.\par
\par
Unlike fossil fuels or biomass, nuclear power does not involve chemical combustion (burning fuel), so it releases zero direct greenhouse gases or carbon emissions during operation.\par
\par
How It Works: Fission vs. Fusion\par
Nuclear energy is generated through two main physical processes:\par
\par
Process\tab How It Works\tab Current Status\par
Nuclear Fission\tab A heavy atomic nucleus (usually Uranium-235 or Plutonium-239) is struck by a neutron, splitting it into smaller fragments. This releases a massive amount of heat, which boils water into steam to drive a turbine.\tab Commercial standard. Powering grids globally today.\par
Nuclear Fusion\tab Light atomic nuclei (such as Hydrogen isotopes) are forced together under extreme heat and pressure to form a heavier nucleus (Helium), releasing huge amounts of energy.\tab Experimental. Currently in advanced R&D stages (e.g., ITER, private fusion startups).\par
Is Nuclear Considered "Renewable"?\par
Nuclear energy is technically classified as clean/sustainable, but non-renewable:\par
\par
Why it's non-renewable: It relies on Uranium ore mined from the Earth, which is a finite resource.\par
\par
Why it functions like renewables: Uranium fuel density is extremely high. A single 10-gram pellet of enriched uranium produces as much energy as 1 short ton of coal or 149 gallons of oil, consuming minimal natural raw material compared to its output.\par
\par
Key Advantages & Disadvantages\par
Pros:\par
\par
Highest Capacity Factor: Operates at peak output ~92% of the time, making it the most reliable 24/7 firm baseload power source available.\par
\par
Zero Operational CO \par
