Explain Small Modular Reactors (SMRs) - how they work, their benefits, and how they differ from traditional nuclear plants.\par
\par
Small Modular Reactors (SMRs) are advanced nuclear fission reactors designed to generate up to 300 MW(e) per unit\f1\emdash roughly one-third the power output of traditional large nuclear power plants\emdash built using prefabricated, standardized modules.\par
\par
Core schematic of a typical Small Modular Reactor, AI generated\par
Core schematic of a typical Small Modular Reactor. Source: VectorMine / Getty Images\par
SMRs vs. Traditional Nuclear Plants\par
Feature\tab Traditional Nuclear Plants\tab Small Modular Reactors (SMRs)\par
Power Output\tab 1,000 MW(e) \endash  1,600+ MW(e)\tab Up to 300 MW(e) per module\par
Construction\tab Custom, multi-billion-dollar on-site assembly lasting 7\endash 12 years.\tab Factory-built in standard units, shipped by rail/truck, installed in under 3 years.\par
Safety Systems\tab Active systems requiring external electrical power, backup diesel, and pumps.\tab Passive safety systems utilizing natural convection, gravity, and heat radiation.\par
Footprint & Siting\tab Requires massive land areas and large bodies of cooling water.\tab Compact footprint; can be sited underground, near remote industrial grids, or retired coal plants.\par
Grid Flexibility\tab Designed primarily for constant, full-power baseload operation.\tab Load-following capabilities; modules can scale up or down to complement intermittent solar and wind.\par
How SMRs Work\par
\par
Like conventional nuclear plants, SMRs use nuclear fission to split enriched uranium atoms, generating heat to convert water into steam that drives a power turbine.\par
\par
However, SMRs integrate key primary components\emdash the core, steam generators, and pressurizer\emdash into a single, compact pressure vessel. The primary coolant loop relies heavily on natural circulation (hot water rises, cool water sinks), eliminating the need for high-pressure external pumps.\par
\par
Passive Safety Design\par
\par
In an emergency (e.g., total grid loss), traditional plants rely on active pumps to prevent overheating. SMRs are designed to shut down automatically without operator intervention or external electricity:\par
\par
Gravity-driven control rods: Automatically drop into the core if electrical power fails.\par
\par
Decay heat removal: Natural convective cooling dissipates excess thermal energy into surrounding passive water pools or the atmosphere indefinitely.\par
\par
Key Advantages\par
\par
Reduced Financial Risk: Standardized factory production lowers capital risk, enabling incremental capacity expansion by adding modules over time.\par
\par
Industrial Decarbonization: Provides high-temperature process heat for industrial applications like green hydrogen production, seawater desalination, and district heating.\par
