Friend now what are the history of nuclear energy accidents\par
\par
The history of major nuclear accidents spans over seven decades. The International Atomic Energy Agency (IAEA) rates events on the International Nuclear and Radiological Event Scale (INES), ranging from Level 1 (anomaly) to Level 7 (major accident).  \par
The Guardian\par
+ 1\par
\par
Timeline of Major Nuclear Accidents\par
Windscale Fire (United Kingdom) \emdash  INES Level 5\par
October 1957\par
A fire broke out in a military graphite-moderated reactor core at Sellafield, burning for three days. It released radioactive iodine-131 into the surrounding environment, leading to a temporary ban on local milk sales to prevent contamination.\par
\par
Kyshtym Disaster (Soviet Union / Russia) \emdash  INES Level 6\par
September 1957\par
A storage tank's cooling system failed at the Mayak nuclear waste reprocessing plant, leading to a non-nuclear explosion. Large quantities of high-level radioactive material were dispersed, requiring the evacuation of tens of thousands of residents.\par
\par
Three Mile Island (Pennsylvania, USA) \emdash  INES Level 5\par
March 1979\par
A combination of mechanical failures and operator error led to a loss of coolant water, resulting in a partial core meltdown of Unit 2. While containment structures prevented significant off-site radiation release, it prompted sweeping overhauls in U.S. nuclear safety regulations and public perception.\par
\par
Chernobyl Disaster (Soviet Union / Ukraine) \emdash  INES Level 7\par
April 1986\par
The worst nuclear accident in history occurred during a low-power safety test on Reactor 4. Flawed reactor design (RBMK type) combined with operator error caused a massive thermal surge, resulting in steam explosions, core rupture, and open graphite fires. It sent radioactive plumes across Europe, forced 220,000+ evacuations, and led to severe environmental containment efforts (the Chernobyl Exclusion Zone).\par
\par
Fukushima Daiichi Disaster (Japan) \emdash  INES Level 7\par
March 2011\par
A 9.0 magnitude earthquake and subsequent 14-meter tsunami inundated the power station. The water flooded basement backup diesel generators, cutting off electrical cooling systems. Decaying heat led to partial meltdowns in three reactors and subsequent hydrogen gas explosions that damaged containment buildings.\par
\par
Key Lessons & Industry Evolution\par
Design Failures vs. External Shocks: Chernobyl highlights the risks of flawed engineering and human error in uncontained designs, whereas Fukushima demonstrated the vulnerability of critical backup systems to extreme natural events.  \par
Wikipedia\par
\par
Safety Overhauls: Modern nuclear reactors (Generation III+ and IV designs, including SMRs) rely heavily on passive safety features\emdash such as gravity-driven control rods and natural cooling circulation\emdash that operate automatically even during complete electrical grid failure.\par
