2. Cost Savings and Comparison per Year\par
\par
Comparing operational cost savings between nuclear and fossil fuels requires looking at fuel costs vs. capital building costs:  \par
World Nuclear Association\par
\par
Fuel Running Costs: Uranium fuel represents only about 15% to 20% of a nuclear plant's operational costs. In contrast, coal and natural gas plants spend 60% to 80% of their annual budget strictly on purchasing fossil fuel.\par
\par
Annual Savings per 1,000 MW Plant:\par
\par
Vs. Natural Gas: A standard gigawatt-scale nuclear plant saves roughly $150M to $300M per year in raw fuel procurement compared to a natural gas combined-cycle plant (subject to natural gas price spikes).\par
\par
Vs. Coal: Saves roughly $80M to $150M per year in fuel costs, while eliminating carbon taxes/penalties in markets with carbon pricing.\par
\par
The Capital Trade-Off: While nuclear is far cheaper to operate annually, it costs $6,000 to $10,000+ per kW to build initially (high upfront capital expenditure), whereas natural gas plants cost under $1,200 per kW to build.  \par
