3. Future Year Projections & Long-Term Financial Estimates\par
\par
Modern financial and energy modeling projects long-term nuclear economics through three main metrics:\par
\par
60-to-80 Year Lifecycle Horizon: Older plants were built for 40-year spans, but current Generation III+ reactors and SMR designs are modeled for 60- to 80-year operational lifespans. Once the initial 15\endash 20 year plant construction debt is amortized, the plant generates some of the lowest-cost clean electricity on the grid for the remaining 40\endash 60 years.\par
\par
System Grid Integration Savings: Recent global energy model calculations demonstrate that including 20\endash 30% firm nuclear power on a grid lowers total system costs compared to 100% intermittent renewables (which require massive overbuilding of battery storage and transmission buildouts).\par
\par
Small Modular Reactor (SMR) Financial Curves: By shifting from custom field construction to factory fabrication, future SMR units aim to reduce upfront capital costs by 30\endash 50% per module, lowering financial risk and shortening construction timelines from 8\endash 10 years down to 2\endash 3 years.\par
